                "quote": "let unsafe (text: string) = text.Contains ',' || text <> text.Trim() || text.Contains '\\n' || text.Contains '\\r'"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Reports.fs",
                "line": 31,
                "quote": "match library.Loans |> Map.tryFind copy.Id with | Some loan -> $\"on loan until {Views.date loan.DueDate}\" | None -> \"on shelf\""
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The branch lookup (Catalog.findBranch), the copy listing (Catalog.copiesAt) and the hold queues (Holds.queueFor) all come from Tier 2. Tier 3 still decides two things itself. It decides which members are 'unsafe' and cannot be exported, which is a validity rule about the import format, and it returns InvalidInput on that basis. It also derives a copy's loan or shelf status directly from library.Loans. Neither contradicts Tier 2, but both are local re-derivations in Tier 3.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "All three new exports write through ports.Files.WriteLines and read state through ports.Store.Load(). The new code adds no clock, file or console access below Tier 4. The CLI only calls Reports.* and renders views. The existing exportOverdue calls File.WriteAllLines directly, but that line is untouched and not part of this change.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Reports.fs",
                "line": 60,
                "quote": "// import-members splits on commas and trims fields, so such values could not be read back."
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Reports.fs",
                "line": 61,
                "quote": "let unsafe (text: string) = text.Contains ','"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The import-members format rules (comma split, field trimming, line-based) are restated as a hand-written 'unsafe' predicate in the exporter. The two agree today, but nothing ties them together, so they could drift. Member type names go through Views.memberTypeName and the date format through Views.date, so those facts stay single-sourced.",
            "score": 1
          }
        },
        "2": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Reports.fs",
                "line": 33,
                "quote": "match Holds.queueFor copy.Book library with | [] -> \"on shelf\" | queue -> $\"on shelf ({queue.Length} holds waiting)\""
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 196,
                "quote": "Catalog.addCopy { Id = CopyId copyId; Book = BookId book; Branch = branchId } library"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The branch rule for adding copies (DEC-011) is enforced by Catalog.addCopy in Tier 2, and Tier 3 only parses rows and sequences the calls. Whether a copy is wanted is answered by Holds.queueFor in Tier 2. The 'N holds waiting' text is projection wording in Tier 3, not a rule about which states are legal.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "importCopies reads the file through ports.Files.ReadLines and persists through the existing commit helper, which goes through the Store port. The all-or-nothing behaviour comes from folding in a Result before the commit, so a failed row leaves the store unchanged. The decision logic is pure and testable without effects. The CLI only dispatches to the use case.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The hold count is derived from the single Holds.queueFor definition. The copy-adding rules are not restated, because they are delegated to Catalog.addCopy. Error text is rendered through Views.describeError, and the 'line N' numbering is defined in one place. No limit, rate or rule is duplicated.",
            "score": 2
          }
